# Switchyard dev server

```curl --insecure -s 'https://switchyard-dev.nvidia.com/v1/chat/completions' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"messages":[{"content":"Hello from Switchyard","role":"user"}],"model":"switchyard/auto","max_tokens": 4096}'```

It has the following endpoints connected to Inference Hub:
* `switchyard/auto`: RSI router. The default route. Uses `recognizer` (Qwen3.8-Flash) to label the step, reads the subtask catalog from Redis, and runs the cheapest candidate from `capable` and `efficient`. Falls back to `capable`.
* `switchyard/composite`: Composite. Decision model as classifier (Mercury Decide), then Stage on tool call signals.
* `switchyard/rsi`: Alias for the same RSI route.
* `switchyard/noop`
* `switchyard/passthrough` -> nvidia/openai/gpt-oss-20b
* `switchyard/random` -> oss-20b (above) and nvidia_dynamo/deepseek-ai/deepseek-v4-flash-nvfp4-elb
* `switchyard/stage` -> nvidia/zai-org/glm-5.2 and aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-4-8
* `switchyard/classifier` -> same models as stage
* `codex-auto-review` -> same model as `switchyard/passthrough`, for Codex "Approve for me" reviews

## RSI router

`switchyard/auto` is the default RSI route. `switchyard/rsi` is an alias with the
same settings. Both read Redis through `RSI_REDIS_URL`. The systemd unit sets
`redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0` by default. Change it if Redis runs elsewhere, or
export the variable before starting the server.

If the variable is unset, the URL is invalid, or a read fails, the server still
starts. The route logs a warning and uses the flagship model for every request.

The catalog is one JSON array at Redis key `rsi:subtasks` by default. Change
the key with `redis_catalog_key`. Each entry needs `subtask_id`, `subtask_name`,
`definition`, and `candidates`. The recognizer sees the ID, name, and
definition, then picks an ID. Candidates use the target model IDs configured in
`[targets]`.

The three RSI logs are enabled by default:

- `logs/rsi-execution.jsonl`: one record per step with the full context,
  execution model response, selected model, usage, cost, latency, and result.
- `logs/rsi-routing.jsonl`: one record per step with the routing decision,
  subtask, selected model, cost, latency, and result.
- `logs/rsi-recognizer.jsonl`: one record per recognizer call with its full
  prompt and raw response.

Override a path with `--trajectory-log-file`, `--rsi-routing-log-file`, or
`--rsi-recognizer-log-file`. Every record carries `request_id`, `ts`, and
`step`, so the three files can be joined. A session ID comes from
`x-switchyard-session-id` or from `metadata.session_id` in the request body.

If you have an NVIDIA UNIX account and Silverfort I think you can ssh to it, ping for details. There's a `/home/README` with the infos once you're in.

The network has some kind of throttling. Not good for evals, but fine for poking around. Only accessible to internal NVIDIA folks for now.

## Files in this folder

- `switchyard.service`. The systemd unit. Goes in `/etc/systemd/system/switchyard.service`. Remember to `systemctl daemon-reload` if you change it. REMEMBER TO ADD THE API KEY. The `switchyard/rsi` route also needs `RSI_REDIS_URL`.
- `config.toml`. The switchyard-server config. Goes in `/etc/switchyard/`.

## Details

This VM ping times maybe vary. `crates.io` appears throttled. Trying to `sudo` will trigger Silverfort.

### Manage

Switchyard is managed with systemd. Config is in `/etc/systemd/system/switchyard.service`.

- `sudo systemctl restart switchyard`
- `sudo systemctl status switchyard`
- `sudo systemctl stop switchyard`

### Logs

- `sudo journalctl -f -u switchyard`

### Update

#### git / binary

```
cd Switchyard/
git pull
export RUSTFLAGS="-Ctarget-cpu=native -Cforce-frame-pointers=yes"
cargo build --release
sudo cp target/release/switchyard-server /usr/local/bin/
sudo systemctl restart switchyard
```

The VM has weird network so have to retry sometimes. Check the status and logs after restarting (see 'Manage' above), because systemd won't tell you that it failed on startup.

#### config

Config is in `/etc/switchyard/config.toml`. Edit it in the repo though, then copy over.

#### TLS

Config and TLS key/cert are in `/etc/switchyard/`.

Re-generate:
```
openssl genpkey -algorithm Ed25519 -out key.pem
openssl req -new -x509 -key key.pem -days 1460 -out cert.pem -subj "/C=US/ST=CA/L=Santa Clara/O=LocalDev/OU=Switchyard/CN=switchyard-dev.nvidia.com"
cp {cert,key}.pem /etc/switchyard/
```

